Feature: Convert Spark.SQL Notebook to PySpark Code

  In order to ensure seamless transition from Spark.SQL to PySpark
  As a data engineer
  I want to convert the Spark.SQL code in the provided notebook to PySpark, including all necessary functions and configurations

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog is set to "purgo_databricks"
    And the Unity Catalog Schema is set to "purgo_playground"
    And the necessary modules "Constants" and "ReusableFunctions" are available
    And all external dependencies and libraries are installed
    And the environment is configured according to requirements

  Scenario Outline: Successful conversion of Spark.SQL to PySpark
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    When the notebook is converted to PySpark code
    Then the PySpark code should include all functionalities from Spark.SQL
    And the PySpark code should use the Unity Catalog "purgo_databricks" and schema "purgo_playground"
    And the PySpark code should correctly reference the modules "Constants" and "ReusableFunctions"
    And the PySpark code should handle the tables "supplier_invoice_orig", "supplier_invoice", "f_supplier_invoice", "supplier_invoice_bkp", "stg_ca_wholesaler", "ods_non_contracting_clone", "stg_nl_wholesaler_clone", "ods_copay_clone", "stg_nl_wholesaler", and "stg_us_wholesaler"
    Examples:
      | notebook_name                      | unity_catalog    | unity_schema     |
      | fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb | purgo_databricks | purgo_playground |

  Scenario: Conversion fails due to missing Constants module
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the "Constants" module is missing
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "Constants module is undefined" should be displayed

  Scenario: Conversion fails due to missing ReusableFunctions module
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the "ReusableFunctions" module is missing
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "ReusableFunctions module is undefined" should be displayed

  Scenario: Conversion fails due to missing external dependencies
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And external dependencies "library_X" and "library_Y" are not installed
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "Missing external dependencies: library_X, library_Y" should be displayed

  Scenario: Conversion fails due to incorrect environment configuration
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the environment is not configured with "spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode" set to "dynamic"
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "Environment configuration 'spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode' is incorrect" should be displayed

  Scenario Outline: Validate output PySpark code for different table configurations
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the table "<table_name>" has columns "<columns>"
    When the notebook is converted to PySpark code
    Then the PySpark code should correctly handle the table "<table_name>" with columns "<columns>"
    Examples:
      | table_name                 | columns                                                                                                                          |
      | supplier_invoice_orig      | src_sys_cd:string, po_nbr:string, po_line_nbr:string, invc_co_amt:double, invc_txn_amt:double, invc_txn_type:string         |
      | supplier_invoice           | src_sys_cd:string, po_nbr:string, po_line_nbr:string, invc_co_amt:double, invc_txn_amt:double, invc_txn_type:string         |
      | f_supplier_invoice         | invc_entry_period:timestamp, suplr_invc_nbr:string, vchr_nbr:string, vchr_line_nbr:string, fscl_yr_nbr:bigint           |
      | supplier_invoice_bkp       | src_sys_cd:string, po_nbr:string, po_line_nbr:string, invc_co_amt:double, invc_txn_amt:double, invc_txn_type:string         |
      | stg_ca_wholesaler          | filename:string, src_sys_name:string, src_product_id:string, src_prod_desc:string, brand:string, customer_id:string         |
      | ods_non_contracting_clone  | PM_ID:string, Shipment_Date:string, Dispense_ID:bigint, DELETE_FLAG:string, Facility:string, Units_Provided:bigint           |
      | stg_nl_wholesaler_clone    | Wholesaler_File_Name:string, Source_System_Name:string, Source_Product_ID:string, Source_Product_Description:string         |
      | ods_copay_clone            | PM_ID:string, NDC:string, Date_of_Service:string, Rx_Claim_Number:string, DELETE_FLAG:string, Copay_Amount:bigint            |
      | stg_nl_wholesaler          | Wholesaler_File_Name:string, Source_System_Name:string, Source_Product_ID:string, Source_Product_Description:string         |
      | stg_us_wholesaler          | Wholesaler_File_Name:string, Source_System_Name:string, Source_Product_ID:double, Source_Product_Description:string        |

  Scenario: Validate error message for unsupported functions in PySpark conversion
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" contains unsupported Spark.SQL functions
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "Unsupported function <function_name> in Spark.SQL" should be displayed

  Scenario: Validate environment variable configurations post-conversion
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is converted to PySpark
    When the PySpark code is executed
    Then the environment variables such as "raw_catalog.schema" and "unity_catalog.schema" should be correctly set
    And the PySpark code should reflect the configurations:
      | Configuration Key               | Value                       |
      | raw_catalog.schema              | <raw_unity_catalog>         |
      | unity_catalog.schema            | purgo_databricks            |

  Scenario: Data integrity check after PySpark conversion
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is converted to PySpark
    When the PySpark code processes the data
    Then the resulting tables "supplier_invoice_orig", "supplier_invoice", "f_supplier_invoice", "supplier_invoice_bkp" should contain identical data as produced by the original Spark.SQL code
    And all columns should adhere to their specified data types and validation rules

  Scenario: Validate handling of missing or null values in PySpark conversion
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is converted to PySpark
    When the PySpark code processes records with missing or null values
    Then the PySpark code should apply the same filtering and defaulting logic as the original Spark.SQL code
    And the resulting data should maintain data integrity without unintended nulls

  Scenario Outline: Handle different load types during conversion
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the load type is set to "<load_type>"
    When the notebook is converted to PySpark code
    Then the PySpark code should handle the load type "<load_type>" appropriately
    And the resulting data should reflect the load type-specific logic
    Examples:
      | load_type      |
      | full_load      |
      | incremental    |
      | batch_processing|

  Scenario: Validate error handling for invalid input parameters
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is provided
    And the input parameters such as "target_table_path" are invalid or improperly formatted
    When the notebook conversion to PySpark is attempted
    Then the conversion should fail
    And an error message "Invalid input parameter: target_table_path" should be displayed

  Scenario: Confirm inclusion of all original SQL queries in PySpark
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" contains multiple SQL queries
    When the notebook is converted to PySpark code
    Then all SQL queries should be translated to equivalent PySpark DataFrame operations
    And the logical flow of data transformations should remain consistent

  Scenario: Validate logging and error tracking in PySpark code
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is converted to PySpark
    When the PySpark code is executed
    Then the code should include appropriate logging for operations and errors
    And error messages should be descriptive and actionable

  Scenario: Ensure PySpark code adheres to performance optimization best practices
    Given the Spark.SQL notebook "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" is converted to PySpark
    When the PySpark code is executed
    Then the code should utilize Spark optimizations such as caching, partitioning, and efficient joins
    And the execution performance should be comparable or better than the original Spark.SQL implementation
